var SaltCore=SaltCore||{};SaltCore["journey-deity-perun"]=(()=>{var n=Object.defineProperty;var s=Object.getOwnPropertyDescriptor;var h=Object.getOwnPropertyNames;var d=Object.prototype.hasOwnProperty;var l=(t,e)=>{for(var a in e)n(t,a,{get:e[a],enumerable:!0})},u=(t,e,a,i)=>{if(e&&typeof e=="object"||typeof e=="function")for(let o of h(e))!d.call(t,o)&&o!==a&&n(t,o,{get:()=>e[o],enumerable:!(i=s(e,o))||i.enumerable});return t};var c=t=>u(n({},"__esModule",{value:!0}),t);var m={};l(m,{journey:()=>r});var r={id:"deity-perun",kind:"deity",paths:["slavic"],deityRef:"traditional/deity/perun",title:"Perun, the Thunderer",summary:"A lunar month with the Slavic god of thunder, oaks and oaths: what the chronicles really say, how he was honoured, and how to keep a plain, honest devotion to him today.",welcome:`This is a month-long self-dedication to Perun, the thunder god of the Slavs. You will read what the few early sources say, learn the storm myth scholars have rebuilt and how much of it is reconstruction, keep a nine-night offering at a small shrine, and decide at the end whether you want to give yourself to his service.

It is a dedication, not an initiation. Nobody is ordaining you, and no community is being joined by default. It is a promise you make yourself, so it asks for one thing above all, which Perun has always asked: that you only say what you mean.

You do not need Slavic ancestry. You need honesty, a little courage, and a willingness to keep your word.`,about:"Perun is the most securely attested god of the pre-Christian East Slavs. A Byzantine historian in the 550s wrote that the Slavs worshipped one god, the maker of lightning, as lord of all. The Rus swore by Perun when they made treaties with Constantinople in 907, 945 and 971, and Prince Vladimir of Kiev set up his wooden image, with a silver head and gold mouth, at the head of a row of idols in 980, before throwing it into the Dnieper at his conversion in 988. His name lives in the Polish word for a lightning bolt (piorun) and is cousin to the Lithuanian Perk\u016Bnas and the Latvian P\u0113rkons. Beyond these facts, most of his mythology, including his famous battle with Veles, is a modern scholarly reconstruction built from folklore, place names and comparison with other Indo-European storm gods. Today he is honoured in Rodnovery, the Slavic Native Faith, at oaks and fires and on his summer feast around 20 July or 2 August, and in Orthodox folk practice through St Elijah the Thunderer.",notes:["Perun is a god of oaths. Do not promise him anything, in this journey or after it, that you are not ready to keep. A small promise kept is worth more to him than a grand one broken.","Most of what you will read online about Perun\u2019s family, wives, weapons and battles is reconstruction or modern invention. The Book of Veles (Velesova kniga), often quoted for him, is a twentieth-century forgery.","Rodnovery is a living, varied religion. Some groups carry nationalist or racial politics, and Perun as a war god has been especially attractive to them. This journey, and this app, are inclusive: the gods are not a bloodline. Choose communities that say so plainly.","Storms are his time, but lightning kills. Every storm practice here is done indoors or after the storm has passed.","His fierce side is real: he is a warrior and a punisher of oath-breakers. The rites here are devotional and protective only; nothing in this journey calls down harm on anyone."],holyDays:["kupala","koliada"],stages:[{kind:"threshold",title:"Standing in the open",intro:"Before reading anything, introduce yourself. You will set a plain space, light a single flame, and tell Perun honestly who you are and why you have come. No promises yet; that comes at the end, if at all.",days:1,lessons:[],practices:[{id:"perun-threshold-journal",title:"Why the Thunderer?",instructions:"After the opening rite, write a page on what drew you to Perun. Be honest if it was a picture, a game, a song, or a storm that frightened you. Note anything you already believe about him and where you learned it, so you can check it against what you read this month.",times:1,prompt:"What do I expect from Perun, and what do I think he will expect of me?"}],rite:{title:"Introduction at the open window",summary:"A short, plain greeting: a flame, bread, a cup, and your name spoken honestly.",minutes:20,ingredients:[{name:"A red or white candle in a sturdy holder",ref:"traditional/candle/red"},{name:"A small piece of bread, ideally rye"},{name:"A cup of kvass, beer, mead, or clean water",ref:"traditional/tool/chalice"},{name:"An oak leaf, acorn or small piece of oak",ref:"traditional/herb/oak",optional:!0},{name:"A plate or bowl for the offering",ref:"traditional/vessel/offering_bowl"}],preparation:"Choose a time when you will not be interrupted. Open a window so the room meets the sky; Perun is a god of the open air and the high places. Wash your hands and face in cold water. Set the candle, bread and cup on a clean cloth on a table or shelf near the window.",steps:[{title:"Stand and face the sky",instructions:"Stand up straight in front of the window. Old Slavic prayer, as far as we can tell, and modern Rodnover practice both favour standing, face lifted, rather than kneeling. Take a few slow breaths and feel the air coming in.",minutes:2},{title:"Light the flame",instructions:"Light the candle. Fire is how Rodnovers most often open a rite to Perun; many say it is because lightning gave people their first fire.",light:!0},{title:"Name him",instructions:"Call him plainly by name. You may use the folk title Gromovnik, the Thunderer.",spoken:`Perun, Thunderer, lord of the oak and the storm,
I greet you with an open window and an open face.`},{title:"Name yourself",instructions:"Tell him who you are: your name, where you live, and in a sentence or two why you have come. Say it in your own words. Do not flatter and do not promise. If you are unsure of yourself, say that; honesty is the whole of it.",minutes:3},{title:"Give the bread and drink",instructions:"Break off a piece of bread and set it on the plate. Raise the cup, pour a little onto the plate or into a bowl as his share, and drink a sip yourself.",spoken:`This is bread from my table and drink from my cup.
I ask nothing tonight but to be known by you.`},{title:"Listen",instructions:"Sit or stand quietly for a few minutes. Notice the sounds from outside: wind, traffic, rain. You are not waiting for a sign, only paying attention.",minutes:5,timed:!0},{title:"Close",instructions:"Thank him, snuff the candle, and close the window.",spoken:"Thank you, Perun. I will come again.",snuff:!0}],closing:"Next day, take the bread and the poured drink outside and put them at the foot of a tree, ideally an oak, or into garden soil. If you cannot, put them in compost; do not eat or drink his share.",safety:"Never leave a candle burning by an open window or near curtains, and never leave it unattended. Keep bread and alcohol away from pets; avoid alcohol if you are under age, pregnant, in recovery or on medication, and use water instead."}},{kind:"study",title:"Learning the Thunderer",intro:"Ten days of reading, a lesson every two days or so, with time to sit with each. You will learn what the sources say about Perun, how the storm myth was reconstructed, how he was worshipped, and how people honour him now.",days:10,lessons:[{id:"perun-who",title:"Who Perun is",summary:"The name, the handful of early sources, and how to tell them apart from later guesses.",minutes:7,sections:[{heading:"The god who strikes",body:`Perun\u2019s name almost certainly comes from an old root meaning to strike or to beat, the same root as the Slavic verb perti. He is, quite simply, the one who strikes. In Polish a lightning bolt is still a piorun, and in older Russian and Ukrainian perun could mean the thunderbolt itself. His Baltic cousins carry close names: Perk\u016Bnas in Lithuania, P\u0113rkons in Latvia.

So when Slavs spoke his name they were naming a thing they could hear and see: the crack over the forest, the split oak, the smell of a struck field. That is a good place to start with him. He is not an abstraction. He is the storm, and the order the storm brings.`},{heading:"What the early writers saw",body:`The oldest witness is a Byzantine historian, Procopius, writing in the 550s about two Slavic peoples on the Danube, the Sclaveni and the Antae. He does not give the god a name, but his description is hard to mistake: \u201CFor they believe that one god, the maker of lightning, is alone lord of all things, and they sacrifice to him cattle and all other victims.\u201D He adds that they honoured rivers and nymphs and other spirits too, and that people in danger vowed a sacrifice to the god if they survived, and paid it.

Four centuries later Perun appears by name in the Russian Primary Chronicle, compiled in Kiev in the early 1100s. When Prince Oleg made peace with the Byzantine emperors in 907, his men, the chronicle says, \u201Cswore by their weapons and by their god Perun, as well as by Volos, the god of cattle, and thus confirmed the treaty.\u201D The same oath by Perun seals the treaties of 945 and 971.

Then comes the most famous entry. In 980 Prince Vladimir, newly master of Kiev, set up idols on the hill outside his hall: \u201Cone of Perun, made of wood with a head of silver and a mouth of gold, and others of Khors, Dazhbog, Stribog, Simargl, and Mokosh.\u201D (Other translators read \u201Cmoustache of gold.\u201D) Eight years later Vladimir became a Christian, and the chronicle tells how Perun was tied to a horse\u2019s tail, beaten with sticks, and cast into the Dnieper while the unbaptized wept.`},{heading:"His forms and names",body:`The sources give us very few titles for him. Folk speech and modern practice add more: Gromovnik or Gromoverzhets, the Thunderer, is the one you will hear most, and Rodnovers sometimes call him Perun Svarozhich, son of Svarog, though that family tree is modern. In the north, near Novgorod, a sanctuary called Peryn stood by the river Volkhov, and the Novgorod chronicle tells of his idol being thrown into that river too.

After conversion, his work passed to St Elijah, Ilya the Prophet, who rides a fiery chariot across the sky in Orthodox folk belief. On Elijah\u2019s day, 20 July in the old calendar and 2 August in the modern one, people prayed against hail and lightning, kept off the fields, and in parts of the Russian north shared a communal feast of a slaughtered ox. Many Rodnovers keep Perun\u2019s day on one of those two dates for exactly that reason.`},{heading:"Telling sources from guesses",body:`It is worth fixing the evidence in your mind now, because the internet will not do it for you. We know his name, that he was a lightning god, that he was invoked in oaths alongside weapons, that he was the chief figure in Vladimir\u2019s row of idols, and that he had sanctuaries near Kiev and Novgorod. Place names such as Perun\u2019s Hill and Perun\u2019s Oak, scattered from Slovenia to Russia, show he was known across the Slavic world.

What we do not have is a single surviving prayer, hymn, or myth told about him by a pagan Slav. Everything else, his wives, his children, his weapons, his day of the week, is built later. Some of it is careful scholarship, some is folk Christianity re-read, and some is pure invention. None of that makes modern devotion false. It does mean you should hold it as what it is.`}],knowAndDont:{known:"Perun\u2019s name and its meaning; Procopius\u2019s sixth-century report of a single lightning god who received sacrifices of cattle; oaths by Perun and weapons in the treaties of 907, 945 and 971; his idol at the head of Vladimir\u2019s pantheon in 980 and its destruction in 988; sanctuaries near Kiev and at Peryn by Novgorod; place names across the Slavic lands; and the transfer of his thunder role to St Elijah.",unknown:"No myth, hymn or prayer to Perun survives from a pagan Slav. His family (Svarog as father, Dodola or Mokosh as wife), his weapons, his sacred day and most of his symbolism are later reconstruction. Scholars still argue whether all Slavs had one shared pantheon, or whether Vladimir\u2019s row of idols was a political creation of his own."},inPractice:"Most Rodnovers address Perun simply by name or as Gromovnik, stand rather than kneel, and open his rites with fire. Write his name and the four or five firm facts above on the first page of a notebook you will keep for him; this is your foundation, and anything you add later goes on later pages.",reflect:["How does it feel to come to a god whose own voice did not survive?","Which of the facts here surprised you, and which belief of yours turned out to be modern?"],further:["The Russian Primary Chronicle, trans. Samuel H. Cross (1930), the entries for 907, 945, 971, 980 and 988","Procopius, History of the Wars VII.14, trans. H. B. Dewing (1924)","Judith Kalik and Alexander Uchitel, Slavic Gods and Heroes (2019): a careful, sceptical modern survey"],library:["traditional/deity/perun","traditional/deity/thor"]},{id:"perun-storm-myth",title:"The storm myth, and how we know it",summary:"Perun and Veles, the oak and the waters: a beautiful reconstruction, and exactly how it was made.",minutes:7,sections:[{heading:"The story as it is usually told",body:`You will meet this story everywhere. Perun sits at the top of a great oak, or on a mountain, in the world above. Below, among the roots and the wet places, lives his adversary, a serpent or shaggy horned being, usually named Veles. Veles steals something from Perun: his cattle, his wife, his son, or the waters. Perun chases him. Veles hides, under a tree, behind a stone, in an animal, in a person. Perun strikes with lightning wherever he hides, splitting tree and rock, until Veles is driven back down to the waters. Then the rain falls, the fields drink, and the world is in order again, until next year.

It is a strong story, and it explains a great deal: why lightning strikes trees, why storms bring rain, why the year turns between dry and wet. It does not, however, survive in any ancient text.`},{heading:"How it was rebuilt",body:`The version you know was assembled in the 1970s by two Soviet scholars, Vyacheslav Ivanov and Vladimir Toporov. They gathered fragments from many places. Folk songs and charms from Belarus, Russia and the Balkans, in which a thunderer pursues a hiding devil. Place names, where a Perun\u2019s hill often faces a Veles-sounding meadow or wet lowland below. Baltic songs of Perk\u016Bnas striking his enemy Velnias. And comparison with other Indo-European myths: Indra splitting the serpent V\u1E5Btra to free the waters in the Vedas, Thor fighting the world serpent, the Hittite storm god and the dragon Illuyanka.

From these they proposed a \u201Cbasic myth\u201D that the early Slavs, they argued, must once have told. The Croatian scholar Radoslav Kati\u010Di\u0107 later added detailed work on South Slavic ritual songs. It is serious scholarship and many specialists accept its broad outline. Others think it joins too many dots, and point out that Veles is never called Perun\u2019s enemy in any medieval source; in the treaties the two gods are sworn by side by side.`},{heading:"What the folklore really keeps",body:`The most solid layer is Christian-era folk belief, recorded by ethnographers in the nineteenth and twentieth centuries. In it the Prophet Elijah, or sometimes St George, hunts the devil across the sky with thunder. The devil hides wherever he can: in a tree, under a cow, in a cat, behind a person who is swearing. That is why, people said, you should not shelter under a lone tree in a storm, and why the cat was put out of the house when thunder came.

Old stone axes and fossil belemnites turned up by the plough were called thunder arrows or thunderstones, and kept in houses as protection against lightning. The six-petalled rosette carved on beams and gable ends across the Slavic and Baltic lands is called the thunder mark by modern writers; villagers called it many things, and its link to Perun is a modern reading, though a widespread one.`},{heading:"What the myth can mean for you",body:`Read as a pattern rather than a history, the story is about order winning water back for the living. It is not good against evil. Veles is not a devil; he is the god of herds, wealth and the underworld, and many Rodnovers honour both. The storm is a quarrel inside the world that keeps the world alive.

That is a useful thing to carry into a devotion. Perun is the one who strikes, but he strikes so that the rain can fall. His justice is meant to restore something, not to destroy for its own sake. If you take on his name, you are taking on that kind of strength: the kind that clears the air.`}],knowAndDont:{known:"Perun and Volos are sworn by together in the tenth-century treaties. Folk legends recorded from the nineteenth century describe Elijah or a thunderer hunting a hiding devil, and stone axes and fossils were kept as thunderstones. Place names often pair a high Perun site with lower wet ground. Baltic and other Indo-European traditions have thunder-god versus serpent myths.",unknown:"No medieval Slavic text tells the Perun\u2013Veles battle. The full \u201Cbasic myth\u201D is a reconstruction by Ivanov and Toporov (1970s), widely used but debated. Whether Veles was ever thought of as Perun\u2019s adversary in pagan times, rather than his partner in oaths, is not known."},inPractice:"Many Rodnovers tell the storm myth at Perun\u2019s summer feast or during the first spring thunder, and honour both Perun and Veles in the same rite, each in his own place: Perun high, by fire; Veles low, by water or the ground. If you tell the story, tell it as \u201Cthe old story as scholars have rebuilt it.\u201D",reflect:["Where in your life is there a quarrel that, once resolved, would let something grow again?","Does it matter to you that the myth is reconstructed? Why, or why not?"],further:["V. V. Ivanov and V. N. Toporov, Issledovanija v oblasti slavjanskih drevnostej (1974), the original reconstruction (Russian)","Radoslav Kati\u010Di\u0107, Bo\u017Eanski boj (2008), on the divine battle in South Slavic tradition (Croatian)","Linda J. Ivanits, Russian Folk Belief (1989), chapters on Elijah and the thunder"],library:["traditional/deity/veles","traditional/herb/oak"]},{id:"perun-worship",title:"How Perun was honoured",summary:"Hills, oaks, weapons and oaths: what we can say about his old worship, and the folk customs that carried it on.",minutes:7,sections:[{heading:"High places and wooden gods",body:`Perun\u2019s known shrines were on rises of ground near rivers. In Kiev, his idol stood on the hill by the prince\u2019s hall. At Peryn, just outside Novgorod, archaeologists in the 1950s found a round sanctuary on a low hill by the Volkhov, with a central post-hole and a ditch shaped like an eight-petalled flower where fires seem to have burned. Whether it was Perun\u2019s is not certain, but the name and the place fit.

His images were wooden, carved from tree trunks, and the Kievan one was dressed in precious metal. The chronicle says people sacrificed to the idols there, and in its angriest line accuses them of offering their sons and daughters. That charge may be Christian polemic, and there is no archaeological proof of it, but you should know it is in the text.`},{heading:"Oaks and offerings",body:`The oak was the tree of the thunder god in many European traditions because it is so often struck. For the Slavs we have one vivid description. The Byzantine emperor Constantine VII, writing around 950 about the Rus traders\u2019 journey down the Dnieper, tells how they stopped on the island of St Gregory, now Khortytsia, at a gigantic oak. There they offered live birds, bread, meat and arrows, cast lots about whether to kill or eat the birds or let them go, and stuck the arrows in the ground around the tree.

He does not name Perun there. But oak, bread, meat and weapons together are exactly the shape later practice takes. An ancient oak trunk with boar jaws set into it was pulled from the Desna river in Ukraine in the 1970s, and many place names join Perun to an oak.`},{heading:"The oath by the weapon",body:`The clearest thing we know about Perun\u2019s worship is the oath. In 945 the Rus who were not Christian laid down their shields, swords and gold before his statue and swore. In 971 Prince Svyatoslav\u2019s oath ends with a curse on himself if he broke it: \u201Cmay we be accursed of the God in whom we believe, namely, of Perun and Volos, the god of flocks, and may we become yellow as gold, and be slain with our own weapons.\u201D

This is the heart of him. An oath by Perun was not a nice sentiment. You put your own weapon, your own safety, under his judgement. That is why the Library warns you not to swear by him lightly, and why this journey keeps every promise small.`},{heading:"What carried on under Elijah",body:`After Christianity, much of his cult moved quietly into the feast of the Prophet Elijah. On Ilyinden people avoided field work so as not to anger the Thunderer, and blessed the first new grain. In parts of northern Russia whole villages bought an ox or bull together, slaughtered it at the church, and ate it in a common meal called a bratchina, a brotherhood feast. Ralston, in 1872, also records that near Kursk and Voronezh a patch of rye was usually left unreaped \u201Cin honour of the Prophet Elijah.\u201D

In Poland, people lit a blessed candle called a gromnica, a thunder candle, during storms. It was blessed on Candlemas, 2 February, the feast of Our Lady of the Thunder Candles. These are Christian customs, and they belong to Christian families. But they show you the old instinct: in a storm, you light a flame, you keep the house quiet, and you ask the sky to pass you by.`}],knowAndDont:{known:"Perun\u2019s Kievan idol on a hill, oaths with weapons laid before his statue (945), the self-curse of 971, the Peryn sanctuary near Novgorod, the oak sacrifice at Khortytsia described by Constantine VII (unnamed god), and the folk-Christian customs of Elijah\u2019s day and the Polish thunder candle.",unknown:"We do not know who led his rites, what words were said, how often they were held, or what his festivals were called. The chronicle\u2019s claim of child sacrifice is unproven. The identification of the Peryn site and the Khortytsia oak with Perun is likely but not certain."},inPractice:"Modern rites for Perun usually borrow these shapes: a high or open place, an oak if there is one, fire, bread and drink, and sometimes tools or blades laid down for blessing. If you keep a knife, a tool you work with, or even a pen on his shrine, you are echoing the oath by the weapon: this is the thing I act with, and I will act rightly with it.",reflect:["What is your \u201Cweapon,\u201D the tool or skill you act in the world with? Would you lay it before a god?","What would you be willing to swear to, if the oath really bound you?"],further:["Constantine Porphyrogenitus, De Administrando Imperio, chapter 9, on the Rus and the oak at St Gregory\u2019s island","Leszek S\u0142upecki, Slavonic Pagan Sanctuaries (1994)","W. R. S. Ralston, The Songs of the Russian People (1872), on Elijah and the harvest"],library:["traditional/herb/oak","traditional/candle/white"],premade:["kitchen-family-meal-blessing"]},{id:"perun-today",title:"Perun today",summary:"How Rodnovers honour him, where communities differ, and where this app stands.",minutes:7,sections:[{heading:"Rodnovery, the Native Faith",body:`Since the 1980s and especially since the 1990s, groups across the Slavic world have revived the worship of the old gods under names like Rodnovery (Native Faith) in Russia, Ridna Vira in Ukraine, Rodzima Wiara in Poland and Rodn\xE1 v\xEDra in Czechia. Perun is honoured in nearly all of them, and in many he is the most important god.

A typical communal rite, a treba (an offering), happens outdoors. People stand in a circle, often around a fire, sometimes around a carved wooden pole of the god called a chur or kumir. A priest or elder, a volkhv or zhrets, calls the gods, the fire receives bread, grain, honey and drink, and everyone drinks in turn from a shared horn or cup. Men\u2019s games, wrestling or mock combat, and the blessing of blades are common at Perun\u2019s feast.`},{heading:"When and where",body:`Most Rodnovers keep Perun\u2019s day around 20 July or on 2 August, the two dates of Elijah\u2019s feast in the old and new calendars. Some honour him at the first thunder of spring, and some at Kupala, midsummer, when bonfires blaze. Thursday has become his day in modern practice. That comes by analogy with Thor\u2019s day and Jupiter\u2019s day: in most Slavic languages Thursday is simply \u201Cthe fourth day.\u201D Only the extinct Polabian language, once spoken in northern Germany, recorded a word for Thursday that is usually read as Perun\u2019s day, and it may have copied German Donnerstag.

Oaks, hilltops and open fields are his places. In a city, a park oak or a rooftop will do.`},{heading:"Where communities differ",body:`Rodnovery ranges from careful reconstruction, working from chronicles, folklore and archaeology, to freely inventive movements with their own scriptures. Some groups use the Book of Veles as a sacred text, though scholars agree it is a twentieth-century forgery. A Ukrainian movement, RUNVira, founded by Lev Sylenko in the 1960s, worships Dazhbog as the one God and gives Perun little place.

There is also a political divide. Some Rodnover groups, in Russia, Ukraine and elsewhere, mix the gods with ethnic nationalism or racial pseudo-history, and far-right armed groups have used Perun\u2019s axe and Slavic sun wheels as badges, including in recent wars. Other groups reject all of that and welcome anyone who comes in good faith.`},{heading:"Where this journey stands",body:`This journey is inclusive and always will be. Perun is not a racial or national possession. The early Slavs were themselves many peoples who mixed with Balts, Finns, Norse, Iranians and Greeks. Anyone who comes to Perun with respect, learns the tradition, and keeps their word is welcome here.

If you look for a group, ask plainly what they think of outsiders, of people of colour, of LGBTQ+ members, and of war. A good group will answer without discomfort. You can also keep a perfectly real devotion alone, at home, as most people in this app will.`}],knowAndDont:{known:"Modern Rodnovery is a twentieth- and twenty-first-century revival with many independent groups. Its common rite, the treba, offers bread, grain, honey and drink to fire, usually outdoors and in a circle. Perun\u2019s feast is commonly kept on 20 July or 2 August after Elijah\u2019s day. Some groups are nationalist or use forged texts; others are inclusive and reconstructionist.",unknown:"Whether Thursday was ever Perun\u2019s day among pagan Slavs is unknown; it is a modern convention. Modern ritual words, the chur pole, and the titles of priests are partly attested in medieval sources and partly reconstructed or invented."},inPractice:"For a solitary practitioner, a simple treba is enough: a flame, bread, drink, his name, your words, and the offering left outdoors afterwards. Thursday is a good weekly day for it, because it is now the shared convention, not because it is ancient.",reflect:["What would you need from a community before you joined one?","How will you answer someone who says Perun belongs only to people of Slavic blood?"],further:["Kaarina Aitamurto and Scott Simpson (eds.), Modern Pagan and Native Faith Movements in Central and Eastern Europe (2013)","Kaarina Aitamurto, Paganism, Traditionalism, Nationalism: Narratives of Russian Rodnoverie (2016)"],library:["traditional/deity/perun","traditional/deity/dazhbog"],premade:["folk-kupala-wreath"]}],practices:[{id:"perun-storm-listening",title:"Listen to a storm",instructions:"When the next thunderstorm comes, stay indoors, away from windows and running water. Turn off music and screens. Count the seconds from flash to thunder (each three seconds is about a kilometre). When the thunder is loudest, name aloud one thing you will stand up for. Wait half an hour after the last thunder before going outside.",times:1,prompt:"What did I name, and did I mean it?"},{id:"perun-find-an-oak",title:"Find your oak",instructions:"Find an oak you can visit: in a park, a churchyard, a street. Learn which kind it is. Look for old lightning scars. Sit with it for ten minutes, then leave a crumb of bread at its roots. This will be where you take his offerings.",times:1,prompt:"What did the tree feel like to be near?"}]},{kind:"practice",title:"Nine nights at the shrine",intro:"Now you build his shrine and keep a short offering on nine nights in a row. Nine is an old and favourite number in Slavic folk charms. You will learn the manner of offering, his days, and what not to do.",days:9,lessons:[{id:"perun-days-offerings",title:"Shrine, days and offerings",summary:"How to set a shrine for Perun in a small home, what to offer, how to offer it, and what to avoid.",minutes:7,sections:[{heading:"A shrine in a small home",body:`Perun likes height and light. If you can, put his shrine high, on a shelf, a windowsill or the top of a bookcase, near a window that faces the sky. In the old Slavic house the holy place was the krasny ugol, the beautiful corner, diagonally across from the stove, where icons now hang. A corner shelf works beautifully.

On it, put something of oak: a branch, a few acorns, a piece of oak wood. Add a candle in red, gold or white, a cup, and a small plate. A stone you found yourself, a flint, or a piece of iron pyrite (which sparks when struck) suits him. Many people add a small axe image or the six-petalled thunder mark drawn on card; you might also lay there the tool you act in the world with. Keep it simple and clean. A cluttered shrine says nothing to a god who values plain speech.`},{heading:"What to offer",body:`The offerings for Perun with the best grounding are bread, meat, drink and fire. Procopius speaks of cattle; Constantine of bread, meat and birds. Modern practice settles on rye bread, honey cake, mead, beer or kvass (a fermented bread drink you can buy in Eastern European shops), and sometimes a little meat or sausage at his feast. Oak leaves and acorns are good gifts for the shrine.

An act is also an offering. Standing up for someone, repaying a debt, telling a hard truth, keeping a promise you would have liked to slide out of: Rodnovers often say these are what he values most.`},{heading:"The manner of offering",body:`Stand. Light the flame first. Call him by name in a clear voice; you do not need old words, only plain ones. Say what you are giving and, if you are asking for something, ask simply and once. Raise the cup, pour his share first, then drink yourself. The poured share and the food are his: do not eat or drink them afterwards.

Leftover offerings go outside, to his oak or onto the earth, within a day. If you burn bread or grain in a fire outdoors, that is the most traditional form of all. In a flat, a few crumbs in a fireproof dish next to the candle and the rest taken to the oak is enough.`},{heading:"What not to do",body:`Do not make him promises you will not keep, and do not swear by him in anger or as a joke. Do not use his name to curse anyone; he punished oath-breakers, but this is not a path for setting him on people.

Do not go out into a storm to \u201Cbe close to him.\u201D Folk belief told people to stay quiet and indoors when Elijah drove his chariot, and modern safety says the same. Do not hug or stand under a tree in thunder, including his oak. And do not fake certainty about him: if you tell others about Perun, say what is known and what is reconstructed. That kind of honesty is itself a gift to him.`}],knowAndDont:{known:"Early sources describe sacrifices of cattle, bread, meat, birds and arrows, oaths with weapons, and worship at high places and oaks. The \u201Cbeautiful corner\u201D is a well-documented feature of the East Slavic house.",unknown:"Exactly how individual households honoured Perun before Christianity is unknown; the shrine setup and Thursday practice here are modern, built from those pieces. Whether kvass or mead were his particular drinks is not recorded; they are simply the traditional drinks of the region."},inPractice:"Set his shrine today. Keep it to oak, flame, cup, plate, one stone and one object of your own. Each night of the next nine, stand before it, light the flame, give a crumb of bread and a little drink, speak to him for a few minutes, and write afterwards.",reflect:["What is the one object of mine that belongs on his shrine?","What act could I offer him this week instead of, or as well as, bread?"],library:["traditional/herb/oak","traditional/crystal/pyrite","traditional/crystal/garnet","traditional/candle/gold","traditional/tool/altar"],premade:["kitchen-courage-brew"]}],practices:[{id:"perun-nine-nights",title:"Nine nights of bread and flame",instructions:"On each of nine nights in a row, stand at Perun\u2019s shrine. Light the candle, call his name, and offer a crumb of bread and a little drink. Speak to him for three to five minutes about one thing: something you need courage for, something unjust you have seen, a promise you are keeping. On the ninth night, offer a whole piece of bread and a full small cup. Snuff the candle each night and take the offerings out to your oak or the earth the next day. If you miss a night, just pick up again; nothing is failed.",times:9,every:"day",prompt:"What did I say to him tonight, and is there anything I said that I now have to live up to?"},{id:"perun-one-just-act",title:"One just act",instructions:"Somewhere in the nine days, do one concrete act of justice or protection: speak up when something is wrong, repay a debt, help someone who is being pushed around, report a hazard. Do it quietly. Tell Perun about it at the shrine that night.",times:1,prompt:"What did it cost me, and what did it give back?"}]},{kind:"discernment",title:"Weighing the oath",intro:"Before any dedication, stop and think. Perun is a god of oaths, so this stage matters more with him than with most. Read the questions slowly, answer them in writing, and give yourself permission to say no or not yet.",days:4,lessons:[],practices:[{id:"perun-discernment-write",title:"Write it out",instructions:"Over several days, answer each of the questions below in your journal. Then reread your threshold entry. Has anything changed? Finally, write the promise you might make him, in one sentence, and live with it for a day before deciding.",times:1,prompt:"If I make this promise, can I keep it in a bad year?"}],questions:["Over these weeks, did honouring Perun make me braver and more honest, or only louder?","Is there a promise I could make him that I am sure I can keep for a year, even when it is inconvenient?","Am I drawn to him for strength and justice, or mostly for the image of a war god? Am I at peace with his fierce side without wanting to use it on others?","Have I found where I stand on the politics in parts of the modern movement, and am I ready to keep my practice inclusive?","Would a quieter god suit my life right now better than the Thunderer?","Is it enough, for now, to honour him without a formal dedication?"]},{kind:"dedication",title:"The oath at the oak",intro:"If you have chosen to go ahead, this is the dedication. It is a self-dedication: a promise you make on your own, to a god, in your own words. It is not an initiation into any Rodnover community or priesthood, and it does not replace one if you later join a group that has its own rites. Keep the promise small enough to keep. Stopping before this point, or choosing another god, is a good outcome too; the oath only means something if it is freely given.",days:1,lessons:[],practices:[],rite:{title:"Dedication to Perun",summary:"A treba at the oak or the shrine: fire, bread, drink, your tool laid down, and one promise you will keep for a year.",minutes:45,ingredients:[{name:"A red candle, or a small fire where fires are allowed",ref:"traditional/candle/red"},{name:"A round loaf of rye bread or a honey cake"},{name:"Mead, beer, kvass or clean water in a cup or horn",ref:"traditional/tool/chalice"},{name:"Oak leaves or acorns",ref:"traditional/herb/oak"},{name:"The tool you act in the world with: a knife, a pen, a tool, an instrument"},{name:"Your written promise, one sentence, on paper"},{name:"A stone, flint or piece of pyrite to keep afterwards",ref:"traditional/crystal/pyrite",optional:!0}],preparation:"Choose a Thursday, his feast day, or simply a clear day. If you can, go to your oak in daylight; otherwise work at your shrine with the window open. Wash in cold water. Check the weather: if a storm is forecast, do this indoors. Write your promise in one sentence, something like \u201CFor one year I will not lie to get out of trouble\u201D or \u201CI will speak up when I see someone bullied.\u201D",steps:[{title:"Make the place",instructions:"Lay a cloth on the ground at the oak\u2019s roots, or clear your shrine. Set the bread, the cup and the oak leaves on it. Lay your tool in the middle, and the folded promise beside it. Walk once around the space, sunwise (clockwise), to mark it."},{title:"Kindle the flame",instructions:"Light the candle or the fire. Stand straight, facing it, with the sky above you.",light:!0,spoken:`Fire from the sky, fire on the earth:
be the door between us.`},{title:"Call him",instructions:"Call him three times, steadily, letting the name carry.",spoken:`Perun! Perun! Perun!
Thunderer, striker, keeper of oaths,
lord of the oak and the rain that follows the storm,
hear me, who has come to you in good faith.`},{title:"Give the bread",instructions:"Break the loaf. Put the first piece by the flame or on the fire, and crumble a little onto the roots or the plate.",spoken:"First bread for you, from a table you have guarded."},{title:"Lay down the tool",instructions:"Put your hand on your tool. Tell him, in your own words, what you use it for and how you mean to use it rightly. This echoes the old oath, when the Rus laid shields and swords before his image.",minutes:3},{title:"Speak the promise",instructions:"Unfold your promise and read it aloud, once, slowly. Then add the words below. Do not add more promises in the moment, however moved you are.",spoken:`This I promise for a year and a day.
I give myself to your honour, Perun,
to keep my word, to stand where I should stand,
and to clear the air rather than poison it.
Witness it, you who hear every oath.`},{title:"Share the cup",instructions:"Pour a generous share from the cup onto the roots or into the offering dish. Then drink from it yourself. You and he have shared a cup; that is the bond."},{title:"Stand in silence",instructions:"Stand quietly with the flame. Notice the air, the sky, your own breath. Let whatever you feel be enough.",minutes:5,timed:!0},{title:"Close",instructions:"Thank him. Take up your tool and your promise; you will keep the paper on his shrine. Put out the flame fully.",spoken:"Thank you, Thunderer. I go with my word kept.",snuff:!0}],closing:"Leave the bread and drink at the oak or take them there the next day. Pick up the stone if you brought one; it is now a token of the oath, and lives on his shrine. Write down the date. A year and a day from now you will renew or release the promise.",safety:"Only light open fires where it is legal and safe, on bare ground or in a fire bowl, with water nearby, and put them out completely. Never perform this outside in a storm or under a tree when thunder is near. Keep candles in sturdy holders away from fabric. Do not leave bread or alcohol where dogs can reach; use water if you avoid alcohol."}},{kind:"ongoing",title:"Keeping faith with the Thunderer",intro:"A dedication is the start of a relationship, not its end. This stage gives you a rhythm for the year: a weekly greeting, his storms and his feast, and a yearly renewing of your promise.",days:3,lessons:[],practices:[{id:"perun-thursday",title:"Thursday greeting",instructions:"On Thursdays, light his candle for a few minutes, greet him by name, give a crumb of bread, and tell him honestly how your promise is going.",times:52,every:"week",prompt:"Did I keep my word this week? Where did I nearly not?"},{id:"perun-feast",title:"Perun\u2019s day",instructions:"On 20 July or 2 August (choose one and keep to it), hold a fuller treba: fire, a round loaf, mead or beer, and if you eat meat, a shared meal with friends. Bless your tools. At midsummer (Kupala) and midwinter (Koliada) light a flame for him as well.",times:1,every:"holy-day",prompt:"What has his strength done in my life this year?"},{id:"perun-rededication",title:"Renew or release the oath",instructions:"A year and a day after your dedication, return to the oak or shrine. Read your promise. Say honestly how you kept it. Either make a new promise for the coming year, or thank him and release the oath. Releasing it cleanly is not failure; breaking it silently would be.",times:1,prompt:"What do I promise now, or what do I lay down with thanks?"}]}],sources:["Procopius, History of the Wars VII.14.22\u201324, trans. H. B. Dewing (Loeb, 1924), quoted: https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Procopius/Wars/7C*.html","The Russian Primary Chronicle, trans. Samuel H. Cross, in Harvard Studies and Notes in Philology and Literature vol. XII (1930; public domain in the US since 2026), entries for 907, 971, 980 and 988, quoted and checked against the scan of that volume (catalogued on archive.org under the volume\u2019s other study, Lieder\u2019s The Don Carlos Theme): https://archive.org/details/doncarlosthemeha0000fred","W. R. S. Ralston, The Songs of the Russian People (1872), pp. 251\u2013252, on Elijah and harvest customs, quoted: https://archive.org/details/songsofrussianpe00ralsuoft","Constantine VII Porphyrogenitus, De Administrando Imperio, ch. 9 (paraphrased)","V. V. Ivanov and V. N. Toporov, Issledovanija v oblasti slavjanskih drevnostej (1974), the storm-myth reconstruction","Radoslav Kati\u010Di\u0107, Bo\u017Eanski boj (2008)","Judith Kalik and Alexander Uchitel, Slavic Gods and Heroes (2019)","Linda J. Ivanits, Russian Folk Belief (1989)","Kaarina Aitamurto and Scott Simpson (eds.), Modern Pagan and Native Faith Movements in Central and Eastern Europe (2013)"]};return c(m);})();
